/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  diafilter03_dspmde_batch  --  batch CONCENTRATION of a two-salt brine on
  the DSPM-DE pore model, and the rejection of a whole campaign stated as
  a TIME average.

  THE OPERATION.  The charge of diafilter01 (10 litres, 0.02 kmol/m3 each
  of NaCl and MgSO4) sits behind 1 m2 of NF270 at 10 bar with NO make-up
  solvent, so the vessel shrinks and both salts concentrate.  The membrane
  is the pore tier of the curated NF270 record (`NF270_dspmde`: pore
  radius, A_k/dx, a constant volumetric charge, a pore dielectric
  constant), and the transport law is DSPM-DE -- steric, Donnan and
  dielectric exclusion with the extended Nernst-Planck equations SOLVED
  across the pore -- asked at every instant of the integration, exactly as
  the spiral module asks it at every station of its channel.  The film is
  the declared k_film with the default film theory (announced).

  WHAT THE RUN SHOWS.  The observed rejection is not a property of the
  membrane alone; it moves with the solution the membrane sees.  As the
  retentate concentrates the NaCl rejection FALLS (0.691 at the start,
  0.554 at the end of this run) while the MgSO4 rejection RISES slightly
  (0.973 -> 0.979).  In DSPM-DE two things push the monovalent salt the
  same way -- a bulk concentration growing past the fixed charge weakens
  the Donnan exclusion, and a growing share of retained divalent ions
  changes what must cross to keep the permeate neutral -- and this run
  does not separate them.  The flux falls with the osmotic
  pressure (244 -> 116 LMH) and the volume reduction reaches 3.9.  Read
  `R_obs_<salt>` in the trajectory against `V_m3`.

  THE REJECTION OF THE CAMPAIGN.  A batch rig is usually judged by the
  permeate it COLLECTED, not by one instant, and two time averages of the
  rejection are published at the end (DEFINED in BatchDiafilter.cpp,
  kpis(), and in docs/ai/membrane-research.md).

  R_avg = 1 - c_permPooled / c_bMeanPerm, where c_permPooled =
  (n_0 - n) / V_perm is the pooled permeate (every drop collected, mixed)
  and c_bMeanPerm = INTEGRAL c_b dV_perm / V_perm is the retentate's bulk
  concentration averaged over what permeated.  It is a weighted mean of
  the instantaneous R_obs (weights c_b Q_p), so it lies between the
  smallest and the largest of them (NaCl 0.635).

  R_avgVsInitialFeed = 1 - c_permPooled / c_bInitial compares the pooled
  permeate with the CHARGE and, because the retentate only concentrates,
  lies below R_avg (NaCl 0.467) -- the number a laboratory gets by
  analysing its collected permeate against the feed it started from, and a
  reminder that it is not the membrane's rejection.
  The steady spiral module states the SPATIAL counterpart: its R_obs is
  the mixed permeate of the whole channel against the inlet feed.

  WHAT IS VERIFIED AND WHAT IS NOT.  Verified (check_diafiltration): the
  DSPM-DE pore model runs inside the batch vessel at every instant;
  matter is conserved to machine precision; the two averages reproduce
  from the published integrals and from the trajectory; R_avg lies inside
  the range of R_obs; the pooled-permeate rejection lies below it, as it
  must when the retentate concentrates.  NOT validated: no number here is
  compared with a measured batch filtration.  The pore parameters are the
  record's case-fitted pedagogical set (`provenance fittedToCase`, fitted
  against membrane10_dspmde_divalent), so the rejections are those of a
  representative loose NF membrane, not a claim about a particular
  element.  In a MIXED feed the salt basis carries each salt's cation leg
  through the pore and the run says so; fouling is not declared; k_film is
  declared, not correlated with a stirrer or a channel.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        retentate;
        type        batchDiafilter;

        operation
        {
            membrane     NF270_dspmde;       // the pore tier of the curated record
            transport    DSPM-DE;            // the solved pore, at every instant
            mode         concentration;      // no make-up: the vessel shrinks

            area         1.0;                // m2 -- membrane area
            P_feed       10 bar;             // transmembrane driving pressure
            P_permeate   0 bar;              // atmospheric permeate side
            rho          1000;               // kg/m3 -- declared, never defaulted

            k_film       1.0e-4 m/s;         // DECLARED: a well-stirred cell

            osmotic
            {
                model    vanHoff;
            }
        }
    }
);
